---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
---

<GuideLayout slug="reduce-discord-ram-usage">
  <div class="gx-guide">
    <div class="gx-hero">
      <p class="gx-hero__kicker">Discord is eating your RAM</p>
      <p class="gx-hero__line">
        Discord's normal Windows client can burn <strong>1 GB+ of RAM</strong> before you even start a
        match. Here's how to claw it back, from 2-second wins to the big one.
      </p>
      <div class="gx-glance">
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">🧠</span> 1 GB+ used</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">⬇</span> down to 288 MB</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">💵</span> Free fixes</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">⏱</span> Most take seconds</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-intro">
      Open Task Manager mid-game and Discord is often using more memory than the game you're playing.
      On a lot of Windows PCs the normal client sits well above <strong>1 GB of RAM</strong> — just to
      show a chat window. Here's why, plus seven things you can actually do about it.
    </p>

    <h2>🔍 Why Discord eats so much RAM</h2>
    <p>
      The normal client carries a large rendering stack and keeps extra processes alive for features you
      may not need while you play. Memory climbs further when it has to keep decoding, animating, and
      syncing a busy Discord session. Then these pile on:
    </p>
    <ul class="gx-nope">
      <li><strong>Hardware acceleration</strong> spins up a GPU process that can leak memory over a long session.</li>
      <li><strong>Animated avatars, emojis, and stickers</strong> get decoded and held in memory as you scroll.</li>
      <li><strong>Big, busy servers</strong> mean more cached members, channels, and media.</li>
      <li><strong>Background telemetry and experiments</strong> add overhead you never see.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🛠 7 ways to cut Discord's memory use</h2>
    <div class="gx-steps">
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">1</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Toggle hardware acceleration</h3>
          <p><strong>Settings → Advanced → Hardware Acceleration.</strong> CPU-bound? Leaving it on shifts
            work to the GPU. GPU maxed in-game? Turning it off frees VRAM and stops a slow memory creep.
            Test both — the right answer depends on your rig.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">2</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Cut the animation load</h3>
          <p><strong>Settings → Accessibility</strong> → turn on <strong>Reduced Motion</strong> and switch
            off animated emoji and stickers. Fewer things looping = less memory used.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">3</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Leave servers you don't use</h3>
          <p>Every server you're in is stuff Discord keeps loaded. Leaving dead ones is the easiest way to
            shrink those cached member and channel lists.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">4</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Clear the cache</h3>
          <p>Fully quit Discord, then empty <code>%AppData%\discord\Cache</code> and <code>Code Cache</code>.
            A bloated cache costs startup memory, not just disk space.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">5</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Actually quit it (don't just minimize)</h3>
          <p>Hitting X just minimizes Discord to the tray — it keeps running. Not in a call? Right-click the
            tray icon → <strong>Quit</strong>. Relaunching takes two seconds and resets the memory baseline.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">6</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Turn off launch-on-startup</h3>
          <p><strong>Settings → Windows Settings</strong> → turn off "Open Discord" on startup. No reason for
            a 1 GB process to load before you've even opened a game.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">7</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Install a lighter client for game sessions</h3>
          <p>For the biggest change, open <a href="/discordmaxxer/">Discordmaxxer</a>, download the Windows
            installer, install it, and sign in with your normal Discord account. It keeps your servers,
            friends, and pings while trimming background work. In a match, press
            <strong>Ctrl + Alt + T</strong> to turn on Tournament Mode; press it again when you want normal
            behavior.</p>
          <p class="gx-note">🧪 Start with a normal message and call before relying on any alternate client in a tournament. This guide covers memory and background load, not every Discord feature.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gx-specs">
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">1,041 MB</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Stock Discord</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">288 MB</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Our lighter build</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">753 MB</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Freed on the same PC</div></div>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-outro">
      <strong>Bottom line:</strong> quit instead of minimize, clear the cache, leave dead servers for the
      quick wins. The bigger win is a lighter client: install Discordmaxxer, sign in normally, and use
      Tournament Mode when the game is open. Same Discord, hundreds of MB lighter on the measured setup.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
